1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
Hello. My name is Abdur-Rahmaan Muhammad Ali, and today I'm presenting RentalPro,

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:10,000
a Digital Rental Management System built across my IT401 and IT402 capstone courses.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,200
Over the next few minutes I'll walk through the problem this system solves,

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:20,400
how it was designed and built, and the results of testing it —

5
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:23,200
including a demo of the working application.

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,200
All six project deliverables — requirements, design, the working prototype,

7
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,000
the full project report, 100% feature completion,

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,200
and the final demo and presentation package — are finished, tested, and documented.

9
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:40,800
Beyond the original requirements, I've also extended the system with a full billing engine,

10
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:44,800
and made it more usable for Harare landlords and tenants,

11
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:48,000
including a choice of English, Shona and Arabic.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,400
Most recently, I translated every screen, message and document into Arabic

13
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:56,800
and hardened the system for production — I'll cover all three.

14
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:01,600
Small landlords and property managers are still running their business on spreadsheets,

15
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:03,600
paper leases, and sticky notes.

16
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:09,200
That creates four real problems: there's no single source of truth for rent and

17
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:13,600
lease data; nothing proactively catches missed rent or an expiring lease;

18
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:17,600
cash payments and verbal maintenance requests leave no accountability trail;

19
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,800
and tenants have no way to self-serve —

20
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,000
they have to call or email for a balance or a repair update.

21
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,200
RentalPro is built for five kinds of users on one platform: Property Owners,

22
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,400
Property Managers, Tenants, Maintenance Staff, and Administrators —

23
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:37,200
replacing the spreadsheet with one accountable system.

24
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,600
Five objectives guided the whole project.

25
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:44,800
First, centralize property and lease data into one record instead of scattered spreadsheets.

26
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:49,600
Second, automate the reminders landlords actually miss — lease expiry, rent due,

27
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:55,200
overdue rent, and overdue maintenance all fire from background jobs, not a human remembering.

28
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,600
Third, give every one of the five roles a scoped view,

29
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:03,200
enforced by role-based access control plus ownership checks —

30
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:06,400
one Owner literally cannot query another Owner's data.

31
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,600
Fourth, make every record auditable: payments are immutable,

32
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:13,600
and every state change writes to an append-only audit log.

33
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,800
And fifth — the one I'll spend the most time proving today —

34
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:20,400
ship it fully tested.

35
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:23,600
Not just working, but 100% of the requirements

36
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:26,400
implemented and covered by an automated test.

37
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:30,000
IT401 Deliverable 1 was the Software Requirements Specification —

38
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
25 use cases across six functional areas: authentication and RBAC,

39
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,800
property and unit management, the full tenant lifecycle from onboarding to self-service,

40
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:43,600
rent and payments including automated alerts, the six-stage maintenance lifecycle, and reporting.

41
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,800
Every one of these 25 use cases —

42
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:52,400
I'll show this again in the testing section — ended up with a route,

43
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:56,400
a template, and at least one automated test behind it.

44
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:00,000
IT401 Deliverable 2 turned those 25 use cases into

45
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,600
a UML class diagram and a 13-table relational schema.

46
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:08,800
Every table uses a UUID string primary key instead of an auto-increment integer,

47
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:14,000
specifically so the identical schema runs unmodified on SQLite in development and test,

48
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,600
and PostgreSQL in production.

49
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:20,800
A few other decisions from this design carried straight through into the build:

50
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:24,400
naive UTC timestamps to avoid timezone bugs across environments,

51
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:29,200
soft status fields instead of physical deletes so history is never lost,

52
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:33,200
rent payments that are only ever inserted and never edited,

53
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:37,200
and JSON columns on the audit log so any entity's

54
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:40,800
before-and-after state can be captured without a schema migration.

55
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:44,000
The application is a layered Flask monolith —

56
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
deliberately simplified from IT401 Deliverable 2's more ambitious microservice sketch,

57
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:53,600
because a single developer needed to build and verify it in the time available.

58
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:58,000
Presentation is 40 Jinja2 templates behind a role-aware top navigation bar,

59
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,600
with a language menu for English, Shona and Arabic.

60
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:07,200
The application layer is 10 Flask Blueprints — one each for auth, the dashboards,

61
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:12,400
properties, tenants, rent, maintenance, admin, reports, the tenant portal, and the REST API.

62
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:18,000
Below that, the domain and service layer holds security.py for RBAC and IDOR protection,

63
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,200
web_security.py for the security headers and the secret-key check, the five scheduler jobs,

64
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:28,800
a small translation module for Shona and Arabic, which also turns Arabic pages right

65
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:34,400
to left, and a services package for PDF generation, Excel export, notifications, and payments.

66
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,800
Data is 18 SQLAlchemy models —

67
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:41,600
the 13 from the design plus 5 for the billing engine —

68
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:46,000
with UUID keys, and the schema is managed by Alembic migrations.

69
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,600
And infrastructure is Docker Compose running PostgreSQL and Redis,

70
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:54,000
with uploads kept on local disk or in an S3-compatible bucket,

71
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
with a dev-mode fallback for every paid external service —

72
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,200
which is what let me build and test

73
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:04,400
this entire system without a single paid account.

74
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,800
Every screen in RentalPro is scoped to one of five roles.

75
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:11,600
Owners create and edit properties and units,

76
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:15,200
configure late fees, and see the full portfolio report.

77
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:19,600
Managers work within properties an Owner already added — onboarding and,

78
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:24,000
new in IT402 Deliverable 2, blacklisting tenants, and assigning maintenance work.

79
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,600
Tenants get a self-service portal: pay rent online, and submit maintenance requests with photos.

80
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:33,200
Staff see only their own assigned work queue —

81
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,800
nothing else in the system is visible to them.

82
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:42,000
And Administrators get full data visibility plus, again new in IT402 Deliverable 2,

83
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,400
a dedicated console for managing every user's role and account status.

84
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:50,000
IT402 Deliverable 2 is where the project went from

85
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,200
working to 100% complete against the original requirements.

86
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:58,000
An audit found eleven gaps, grouped on this slide into eight areas:

87
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,600
photo uploads for properties, units, and maintenance tickets, all validated by their actual file

88
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:09,200
content, not just the extension; editing a unit after it's created; configurable late fees,

89
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:13,600
fixed or percentage, applied automatically once rent is overdue; tenant blacklisting,

90
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:17,600
which blocks a future lease without touching an existing one;

91
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:22,000
tenant ID documents and lease document downloads; the admin user console,

92
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,200
with a safety guard that won't let you remove the last active Owner;

93
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:31,200
self-service staff account creation; and one-click CSV and Excel exports.

94
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:34,800
All eleven are covered by a dedicated 31-test module,

95
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:37,200
which I'll show numbers for next.

96
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:41,600
When I later traced all 44 functional requirements one by one,

97
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:46,800
two were only partly met — the full tenant history of each unit,

98
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:52,400
and a maintenance summary by period — and the interface languages weren't built yet.

99
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:56,800
I built all three, and all 44 requirements are now met.

100
00:06:56,800 --> 00:07:02,000
Beyond the original requirements, I extended RentalPro with a full billing engine —

101
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:07,600
eight more capabilities, each additive so the existing 124 tests never had to change.

102
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:12,000
Operational and sundry charges bill one-off costs straight to a lease.

103
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,600
Electricity metering bills consumption automatically from a configured rate.

104
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:21,200
Pro rata rent prorates a partial first month instead of charging it in full.

105
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:24,800
Variable rent lets a lease's rate change mid-term —

106
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:30,000
an annual escalation — while past periods stay billed at their own rate.

107
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,400
A daily scheduler job now bills each period automatically and idempotently.

108
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:39,600
Account statements support a real date range with an opening and closing balance,

109
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:42,000
and tenants can view their own.

110
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,200
Every payment gets a real downloadable PDF receipt.

111
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:49,200
And property expenses now feed a true net income estimate,

112
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:51,600
not just rent collected minus maintenance.

113
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:54,400
Seventy-nine new tests back all of it,

114
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:58,000
and they're included in the testing numbers coming up.

115
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:02,400
The most recent round of work was about the people who

116
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:06,800
would actually use RentalPro — landlords, managers and tenants in Harare.

117
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:11,600
Every property can now carry map coordinates, set by clicking a map,

118
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:15,200
and there's a portfolio map showing all of them.

119
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:19,600
The interface moved from a sidebar to a horizontal top bar,

120
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:22,800
and the login page and each section of

121
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:25,600
the app now show photographs of Harare.

122
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:30,000
The biggest change is language: a menu in the top bar

123
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,000
and on the login page offers English, Shona and Arabic,

124
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
and the choice sticks when you log in and out.

125
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:42,400
My SRS originally described Arabic and English; both are now in,

126
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:47,200
and because the users are in Harare, I added Shona as well.

127
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,800
Arabic is written right to left, so an Arabic page mirrors the whole layout.

128
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:58,000
Only interface text is translated — names and places stay exactly as entered,

129
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,200
and a test fails if that ever changes.

130
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:06,800
Tenants now see their unit as 'Avondale Heights, Unit 101' rather than an internal

131
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:10,400
code, a blank-page bug for signed-in users is fixed,

132
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:14,400
and the demo data is now a realistic Harare portfolio:

133
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:17,200
five properties, sixteen units and nine tenants.

134
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,600
Twenty-seven new tests cover this work.

135
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,800
The last round of work finished two things:

136
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:25,600
the language support, and readiness for production.

137
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:28,800
Before it, Arabic covered the main screens only.

138
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:33,200
Now every page, form option and message is in Arabic —

139
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:35,200
over six hundred phrases —

140
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:40,400
and a test fails if any new text is added without a translation.

141
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:44,400
Emails and SMS are written in each user's saved language,

142
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:47,200
even when a nightly job sends them.

143
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:52,800
PDFs are fully Arabic too: the letters are joined and read right to left,

144
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:58,000
and tables are mirrored, while names, dates and amounts keep their own order.

145
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,600
Every amount in the system now shows its currency.

146
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:06,000
On the production side, every response carries strict security headers —

147
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:10,000
a content policy with no inline code at all —

148
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,400
the session cookie is locked down,

149
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:18,000
and the app simply refuses to start with a missing or published secret key.

150
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:23,200
Alembic migrations replace create_all, and existing databases are adopted with their data intact.

151
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:28,800
And uploads can be kept in an S3-compatible bucket, so they survive a redeploy.

152
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:34,000
Fifty-four new tests cover this round, and seven more cover the Render deployment,

153
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,400
where render.yaml deploys the app and its PostgreSQL database over HTTPS.

154
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,400
Here's what that looks like.

155
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:46,000
On the left is the login page: a full-screen photograph of the Harare skyline,

156
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,800
a translucent sign-in card, and the language menu open in the corner,

157
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:52,800
offering English, ChiShona and Arabic.

158
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:56,400
On the right is a tenant's dashboard in Arabic.

159
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:02,000
The whole layout reads right to left — the navigation starts on the right,

160
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,400
and the cards and tables are mirrored — with a welcome,

161
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:10,400
her unit, her balance, and the button to pay rent.

162
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:14,000
Her name and 'Avondale Heights' stay exactly as entered,

163
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,400
because they're data, not interface text.

164
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:21,200
This is the slide that backs up every claim in this presentation.

165
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:26,000
305 automated tests, zero failures, 86 percent statement coverage across the backend,

166
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,600
and all 25 use cases from the SRS covered by at least one test.

167
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:36,000
The chart breaks that down by module — 36 validation tests,

168
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:41,200
31 for IT402 Deliverable 2's feature gaps, 79 for the billing engine alone,

169
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:46,800
the single largest block in the project, 22 for the property map and the

170
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:52,400
three languages, 13 for the tenant history and maintenance summary screens that completed two

171
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:58,000
of the last three requirements, and 54 for the final round — full Arabic,

172
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:03,600
security headers, migrations, currency and cloud storage — and 7 for the Render deployment.

173
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:09,200
Importantly, these aren't unit tests calling isolated functions — they go through Flask's real

174
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:14,800
test client, issuing actual HTTP requests through session handling, CSRF, RBAC, and the ORM.

175
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:19,200
That's how a real security bug like cross-owner data access —

176
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:22,400
one Property Owner querying another Owner's property —

177
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:25,200
gets caught before it ships, not after.

178
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:27,200
Now let's see it running.

179
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:30,000
The demo video walks through six things.

180
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,600
First, the administrator's Harare portfolio: the dashboard, the five properties, and the portfolio map.

181
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:40,800
Second, the tenants list and the rent tracker, where a cash payment clears

182
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:45,600
a tenant's arrears and gets a receipt number and an audit-log entry.

183
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:50,400
Third, an assigned maintenance ticket, and the reports page with rent collected,

184
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:53,200
expenses and net income for each property.

185
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:57,200
Fourth, the language menu: the whole interface switches to Shona,

186
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:00,400
then to Arabic, laid out right to left,

187
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:05,600
and the choice is remembered on the login page and on the account.

188
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:09,600
Fifth, a tenant's portal in Shona, then, as the owner,

189
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:13,600
the tenant history of a unit and the maintenance summary,

190
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:16,800
the two screens added after IT402 Deliverable 2.

191
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:21,200
And finally, the property manager working in Arabic: a tenant profile,

192
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:25,200
a rent statement with charges, the meter and rent revisions,

193
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:29,200
a payment confirmation, and the statement as an Arabic PDF.

194
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:30,800
Three challenges stand out.

195
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:35,600
The first was reconciling earlier, more ambitious design documents with what one

196
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:40,000
developer could actually build and verify in the time available —

197
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:44,400
I simplified IT401 Deliverable 2's microservice-style sketch into a layered monolith,

198
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:49,600
and when I found IT401 Deliverable 3's original numbers had drifted from reality,

199
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:54,000
I corrected the document rather than leaving the gap in place.

200
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,600
Second, getting recurring billing correct at calendar edges —

201
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:03,200
a lease starting on the 31st, or a due day that doesn't exist in

202
00:14:03,200 --> 00:14:08,400
February — meant writing the edge-case tests before trusting the calculation, not after.

203
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:13,600
Third, testing payments, email, and SMS without a live paid account of any

204
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:19,200
kind — solved with a dev-mode fallback pattern applied consistently across every external integration,

205
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:24,000
which is also exactly what let this whole demo run offline today.

206
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:28,400
Security wasn't bolted on — these controls run through every request.

207
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:31,600
Passwords are hashed with 600,000 rounds of PBKDF2,

208
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:36,000
and the app refuses to start without a real secret key,

209
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
since a published one would let anyone forge a login.

210
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,600
Every protected route carries a role check, and every sensitive lookup also carries an

211
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:51,200
ownership check at the resource level — the combination is what stops a second

212
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:56,800
Property Owner from viewing the first Owner's data even though they share a role.

213
00:14:56,800 --> 00:15:02,400
CSRF protection covers every form, with one deliberate, documented exemption for the Stripe webhook,

214
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:07,200
which instead carries its own manual HMAC verification with a replay window.

215
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:12,400
The session cookie can't be read by scripts or sent on cross-site posts.

216
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:16,000
Every response carries a strict content security policy —

217
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:21,200
no inline code at all — blocks framing, and enables HSTS over HTTPS.

218
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:24,800
Five failed logins locks an account for 15 minutes.

219
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:30,400
Every file upload is validated by its actual magic bytes, not its claimed extension.

220
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:34,400
And every state-changing action writes to an immutable audit log.

221
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:38,800
To wrap up: RentalPro went from a requirements document, through design,

222
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:43,600
to a working prototype, a full seven-chapter project report, 100% feature completion,

223
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:47,200
an extended billing engine, a localised interface for Harare,

224
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:50,400
and finally full Arabic and production hardening —

225
00:15:50,400 --> 00:15:53,600
and every number on this slide was produced

226
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:56,400
by actually running the code, not estimated.

227
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:01,600
25 of 25 use cases, each with a route and a covering test.

228
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:04,400
The 13-table design implemented exactly as specified.

229
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:07,200
305 tests, zero failures, 86 percent coverage.

230
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:10,400
Eleven feature gaps closed in IT402 Deliverable 2,

231
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:14,800
eight more billing capabilities added since, and an interface in English,

232
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:20,000
Shona and Arabic — with Arabic reaching every screen, message, email and PDF.

233
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,800
And the whole thing runs fully offline,

234
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:27,200
with zero paid external accounts required to build or test it.

235
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:32,000
That discipline — that a claim in the report is a fact

236
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:36,800
about the system, not a target for it — is, I'd argue,

237
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:41,200
as much a deliverable of this capstone as the application itself.

238
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:45,600
Looking beyond IT402. First, native-speaker review: the Arabic now covers everything,

239
00:16:45,600 --> 00:16:48,000
and Shona covers the main screens,

240
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:52,800
so the next step is for Arabic and Shona speakers to check

241
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:57,600
the wording and for Shona to reach the rest of the app.

242
00:16:57,600 --> 00:16:59,600
Second, a mobile-optimized tenant portal,

243
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:04,800
since tenants are the role most likely to use this from a phone.

244
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:09,600
Third, live production use: render.yaml already deploys the app and its PostgreSQL

245
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:14,400
database to Render over HTTPS, but running it with real tenants still

246
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:19,200
needs uploads in an S3 bucket, since the server's own disk doesn't

247
00:17:19,200 --> 00:17:24,000
survive a redeploy, and a database plan that keeps data long term.

248
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,400
Fourth, end-to-end verification against live Stripe, SendGrid, and Twilio sandbox accounts,

249
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:33,600
on top of the offline test suite that's carried the project so far.

250
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:37,200
And last, two-factor sign-in for Owners, Managers and Administrators,

251
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:40,400
the roles that can see the most data.

252
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:43,200
Thank you. That's RentalPro — a fully-implemented,

253
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:48,400
fully-tested digital rental management system covering all six deliverables from requirements through the

254
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:53,200
final demo and presentation package, plus an extended billing engine on top.

255
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:58,000
I'm happy to take any questions — about a specific design decision,

256
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:03,600
the test suite, the security model, or anything you'd like to see demonstrated again.

